backport_kernel_connect() declares struct sockaddr_unsized inside its
parameter list. A tag declared in a parameter list has prototype scope
and creates a distinct type, which then clashes with the kernel's own
file-scope struct sockaddr_unsized (in-kernel since 6.18.57) at call
sites that cast to it (ath12k/qmi.c), failing -Werror builds with
"incompatible pointer type" on identical type names. This happens even
though backport-include/linux/socket.h no longer redefines the struct
on 6.18.57+, because linux/net.h is typically included from within the
kernel's socket.h before the struct definition is reached.

Forward-declare the tag at file scope before the function so the
parameter refers to the same type everywhere.

--- a/backport-include/linux/net.h
+++ b/backport-include/linux/net.h
@@ -7,6 +7,12 @@
 
 #if LINUX_VERSION_IS_LESS(6,19,0)
+/*
+ * Forward-declare the tag at file scope: a tag declared inside the
+ * function's parameter list has prototype scope and creates a distinct
+ * type that clashes with the kernel's own definition (since 6.18.57).
+ */
+struct sockaddr_unsized;
 /*
  * The in-kernel socket API takes struct sockaddr_unsized since 6.19.
  * Older kernels still expect struct sockaddr here.
  */
